questions.model_answer:
**C2 is the setup phase:** PSEL is high and PENABLE is low, so PADDR, PWRITE and PWDATA must already be valid. SETUP always lasts exactly one cycle (IHI0024E §4.1).

**C3 and C4 are the access phase:** PENABLE is high. In C3 the completer holds PREADY low, which is **one wait state**. In C4 PREADY is high, so the transfer **completes at edge 4**, the first edge at which PSEL, PENABLE and PREADY are all high (§3.1.2, §4.1). Every APB transfer takes at least two cycles; this one takes three.

While PREADY is low, PADDR, PWRITE, PSEL, PENABLE, PWDATA, PSTRB and PPROT (and any user signals) must not change (§3.1.2). PREADY may take any value while PENABLE is low, so the x in C2 is legal; that lets a fixed two-cycle peripheral tie PREADY high. PSLVERR is only considered valid in the last cycle, when PSEL, PENABLE and PREADY are all high, so only C4's value counts (§3.4).

After completion PENABLE drops. PSEL drops too, unless another transfer to the same peripheral follows: then the bus goes straight to SETUP with PSEL still high and PENABLE low (§4.1).

A monitor therefore records a transfer on the edge where all three are high:

```systemverilog
// snippet: inside an APB monitor, evaluated at each rising PCLK edge (via a clocking block)
if (vif.psel && vif.penable && vif.pready) begin
  item.addr  = vif.paddr;
  item.write = vif.pwrite;
  item.data  = vif.pwrite ? vif.pwdata : vif.prdata;
  item.err   = vif.pslverr;   // valid only in this cycle (IHI0024E §3.4)
  ap.write(item);
end
```

The bus is pipelined. By the time a slave decides to signal an error, the address of the next transfer is already on the bus. The two-cycle response gives the master time to cancel that next access and drive HTRANS to IDLE before it starts (IHI0033C §5.1.3). The cycles are (Table 5-2): first ERROR cycle HRESP = 1, HREADY = 0; second ERROR cycle HRESP = 1, HREADY = 1. A slave that needs longer inserts wait states first, and during those HREADY is low and HRESP **must be OKAY** (§5.1.3).

The colleague's property checks only "a first cycle is followed by a second". A one-cycle ERROR (HRESP = 1 with HREADY = 1, no first cycle) never matches its antecedent, so it passes vacuously. Add the backward direction:

```systemverilog
// Bus-level checks on HREADY and HRESP after the multiplexor (IHI0033C §5.1.3, Table 5-2)
property p_err_first_then_second;
  @(posedge HCLK) disable iff (!HRESETn)
    (HRESP && !HREADY) |=> (HRESP && HREADY);
endproperty

property p_err_second_needs_first;
  @(posedge HCLK) disable iff (!HRESETn)
    (HRESP && HREADY) |-> $past(HRESP && !HREADY);
endproperty

a_err_fwd: assert property (p_err_first_then_second);
a_err_bwd: assert property (p_err_second_needs_first);
```

Together they accept OKAY waits, then exactly one ERROR cycle with HREADY low, then the completing ERROR cycle, and they also accept back-to-back ERROR pairs. They reject a one-cycle ERROR, a pair cut short, and an ERROR that stretches with HREADY low for two cycles.

After the first ERROR cycle the master **may** cancel the rest of a burst, and if it does it must drive HTRANS to IDLE during the two-cycle response; continuing the burst is also allowed, and a cancelled burst need not be rebuilt later (§3.6.2). So "HTRANS must be IDLE after an ERROR" is not a valid check, but "if the next beat is cancelled, HTRANS is IDLE" is.

The monitor mirrors the bus pipeline. At each rising edge where HREADY is high, two things happen in this order: the transfer whose data phase is in progress completes, and the address phase on the bus is accepted (IHI0033C §3.1, §4.2). When HREADY is low nothing completes and nothing is accepted.

```systemverilog
// snippet: run_phase of class ahb_monitor extends uvm_monitor
// vif.mon_cb is a clocking block @(posedge HCLK) with every bus signal as an input.
task run_phase(uvm_phase phase);
  ahb_item dp;              // transfer whose data phase is in progress
  bit      dp_valid = 0;
  forever begin
    @(vif.mon_cb);
    if (!vif.mon_cb.HRESETn) begin dp_valid = 0; continue; end
    if (!vif.mon_cb.HREADY) continue;           // wait state: nothing completes or starts

    // 1. The data phase in progress ends at this edge.
    if (dp_valid) begin
      dp.data = dp.write ? vif.mon_cb.HWDATA : vif.mon_cb.HRDATA;
      dp.resp = vif.mon_cb.HRESP;               // 1 here = second ERROR cycle
      ap.write(dp);
      dp_valid = 0;
    end

    // 2. The address phase on the bus is accepted at this edge (NONSEQ or SEQ only).
    if (vif.mon_cb.HTRANS[1]) begin
      dp = ahb_item::type_id::create("dp");
      dp.addr  = vif.mon_cb.HADDR;
      dp.write = vif.mon_cb.HWRITE;
      dp.size  = vif.mon_cb.HSIZE;
      dp.burst = vif.mon_cb.HBURST;
      dp.trans = vif.mon_cb.HTRANS;
      dp_valid = 1;
    end
  end
endtask
```

Why each case works:

1. **Wait states.** The slave holds HREADY low, so the loop skips those edges. The master holds HWDATA valid until HREADY is high (§6.1.1), and a slave need only drive HRDATA in the completing cycle (§3.1), so sampling at the completing edge is right for both.

2. **IDLE and BUSY** have HTRANS[1] = 0 and are never captured. The slave must give them a zero-wait OKAY (§3.2), so their data phases carry nothing to record.

3. **ERROR.** The first ERROR cycle has HREADY low (skipped). At the second, HREADY is high with HRESP high, so the item completes with resp = ERROR (§5.1.3, Table 5-2). If the master cancelled the next transfer, HTRANS is IDLE at that edge, so nothing is captured. If it continued the burst, the next beat is captured normally.

4. **Changes during waits.** The master may change HTRANS from IDLE to NONSEQ, and in some BUSY cases the address, during a wait (§3.7). The monitor only trusts values at the HREADY-high edge, so those changes are handled.

Bursts: group a NONSEQ with the following SEQs. An undefined-length INCR ends at the next NONSEQ or IDLE (§3.6.1), and any burst can stop early after an ERROR or a multi-layer interconnect termination (§3.6.2). Compare transfers individually in the scoreboard, not whole bursts. For a slave-side monitor, also require HSELx at the accepting edge.

Use the equations of IHI0022E A3.4.1: Number_Bytes = 2^AxSIZE, Burst_Length = AxLEN + 1, Aligned_Address = INT(Start / Number_Bytes) x Number_Bytes, Address_N = Aligned_Address + (N - 1) x Number_Bytes for N > 1 (INCR, and WRAP before it wraps).

**(a) INCR at 0x1002.** Number_Bytes = 4, Aligned_Address = 0x1000. Beat 1 is 0x1002; beats 2-4 are 0x1004, 0x1008, 0x100C. Byte lanes for beat 1: Lower_Byte_Lane = 0x1002 - 0x1000 = 2 and Upper_Byte_Lane = 0x1000 + 3 - 0x1000 = 3, so lanes 2-3. Beats 2-4 use lanes 0-3. The burst moves 2 + 4 + 4 + 4 = 14 bytes, not 16.

**(b) WRAP at 0x38.** Total size = 4 x 4 = 16 bytes, Wrap_Boundary = INT(0x38 / 16) x 16 = 0x30. Beats: 0x38, 0x3C, then 0x40 equals boundary + 16, so it wraps to 0x30, then 0x34. All lanes 0-3 on every beat. The start is aligned to the size and the length is 4, so the burst is legal.

WSTRB must be high only for lanes that hold valid data (A3.4.3), so on beat 1 of (a) it is any subset of 4'b1100, including 4'b0000.

```systemverilog
typedef enum logic [1:0] {FIXED = 2'b00, INCR = 2'b01, WRAP = 2'b10} axi_burst_e;

// Address of beat n (n = 1 is the first beat), from the IHI0022E A3.4.1 equations
function automatic logic [63:0] axi_beat_addr(logic [63:0] start, logic [2:0] size,
                                              logic [7:0] len, axi_burst_e burst,
                                              int unsigned n);
  longint unsigned nbytes  = 64'd1 << size;
  longint unsigned total   = nbytes * (len + 1);     // bytes in the whole burst
  logic [63:0]     aligned = (start / nbytes) * nbytes;
  logic [63:0]     wrap_lo = (start / total) * total;
  logic [63:0]     addr;
  if (n == 1 || burst == FIXED) return start;
  addr = aligned + (n - 1) * nbytes;                   // INCR, and WRAP before wrapping
  if (burst == WRAP && addr >= wrap_lo + total)
    addr -= total;                                     // back to the wrap boundary
  return addr;
endfunction
// axi_beat_addr(64'h1002, 2, 3, INCR, 2) == 64'h1004
// axi_beat_addr(64'h38,   2, 3, WRAP, 3) == 64'h30
```

The expression `len + 1` is evaluated at 64 bits here because the multiplication's other operand is 64 bits, so AxLEN = 255 gives 256, not 0.

Illegal. 8 beats x 4 bytes = 32 bytes. Start address 0x0FF0, last byte address = 0x0FF0 + 31 = 0x100F. Start page = 0x0FF0[31:12] = 0x0; end page = 0x100F[31:12] = 0x1. The burst crosses the 4KB boundary at 0x1000 (IHI0022E A3.4.1: a burst must not cross a 4KB address boundary, which keeps a burst from spanning two slaves).

The master must issue it as two bursts: 4 beats at 0x0FF0 (ending at 0x0FFF) and 4 beats at 0x1000.

A checker for this rule applies only to INCR bursts, because a legal WRAP burst stays inside its own wrap block (at most 16 x 128 bytes, aligned) and a FIXED burst repeats one address. For an unaligned start, compute the last byte from the aligned address: last = Aligned_Address + Burst_Length x Number_Bytes - 1.

```systemverilog
function automatic bit axi_crosses_4kb(logic [63:0] addr, logic [7:0] len, logic [2:0] size);
  logic [63:0] aligned = (addr >> size) << size;
  logic [63:0] last    = aligned + ((64'(len) + 1) << size) - 1;
  return addr[63:12] != last[63:12];
endfunction
// axi_crosses_4kb(64'h0FF0, 7, 2) == 1
```

**Sequence (IHI0022E A7.2.1).** The master performs an exclusive read (AxLOCK set). Later it performs an exclusive write to the same address with an AWID that matches the ARID of the read. The write succeeds if no other master has written that location since the exclusive read, and fails otherwise, in which case memory is not updated.

**Master's view (A7.2.2).** EXOKAY on the read means the slave recorded the address. OKAY on the exclusive read means the slave does not support exclusives; the specification recommends the master does not then perform the write. EXOKAY on the write is success; OKAY is failure. The master must not start the write until the read has completed.

**Slave's view (A7.2.3).** A slave without exclusive support ignores AxLOCK and returns OKAY. A slave with support has a monitor that records the address and ARID of each exclusive read and watches the location until a write occurs there, or until another exclusive read with the same ID moves it. An exclusive write whose address is no longer monitored for that ID gets OKAY and does not update memory.

**Restrictions (A7.2.4)**, otherwise behaviour is UNPREDICTABLE: the write has the same size, length and control signals as the read, with an identical address; the address is aligned to the total bytes; the total is a power of two up to 128 bytes; at most 16 beats in AXI4; and AxCACHE must guarantee the monitoring slave sees the transaction (not Cacheable). AXI4-Lite has no exclusives (B1.1); AXI4 removed locked transfers (A7.3).

```systemverilog
// snippet: members of an exclusive-access reference model in the scoreboard
typedef struct { bit valid; bit [63:0] addr; int unsigned nbytes; } excl_mon_t;
excl_mon_t mon[bit [7:0]];                 // one monitor per ID (A7.2.3)

function void on_excl_read_done(bit [7:0] id, bit [63:0] addr, int unsigned nbytes);
  mon[id] = '{valid: 1'b1, addr: addr, nbytes: nbytes};   // re-arms this ID
endfunction

// Call before applying the write to the memory model
function bit [1:0] predict_excl_write(bit [7:0] id, bit [63:0] addr);
  if (mon.exists(id) && mon[id].valid && mon[id].addr == addr) return 2'b01; // EXOKAY
  return 2'b00;                                                           // OKAY, no update
endfunction

// Every completed write that updates memory, exclusive or not, clears overlapping monitors
function void on_write_done(bit [63:0] addr, int unsigned nbytes);
  foreach (mon[id])
    if (mon[id].valid && addr < mon[id].addr + mon[id].nbytes && mon[id].addr < addr + nbytes)
      mon[id].valid = 0;
endfunction
```

A real monitor may watch more bytes than the access, up to 128, so a neighbouring write can make a correct exclusive fail (A7.2.4). Configure the model with the DUT's monitor granularity, or let it accept OKAY where it predicted EXOKAY when only a neighbouring write intervened.

Scenarios to cover: a clean pair (EXOKAY, EXOKAY); another master writes between the pair (OKAY, no update); the same ID re-arms with a new exclusive read; two IDs race for one location; an exclusive to a slave without support; an exclusive write with no preceding exclusive read (the model predicts OKAY with no update).

**Split the work.** Assertions own cycle-level, interface-local rules. The scoreboard owns anything that needs a memory model or matches transactions across channels and IDs.

**Assertions (AXI rules, IHI0022E):**

1. VALID held and payload stable while VALID is high and READY low, on all five channels (A3.2.1).

2. RVALID only for an accepted AR; BVALID only after the AW handshake and the last W beat (A3.3.1, AXI4 write response dependency). These need small outstanding counters or a FIFO of AWLEN values.

3. Burst legality on AW and AR: WRAP length 2, 4, 8 or 16 with a size-aligned start; FIXED at most 16 beats; INCR at most 256 beats; AxSIZE not wider than the bus; no 4KB crossing, checked for INCR only (A3.4.1).

4. WLAST on beat AWLEN + 1 of each write, taking W in AW order (A5.4.1), and RLAST on the last beat of each read.

5. Exclusive-pair restrictions where the port supports exclusives (A7.2.4).

**Policy checks (label them):** no X on VALID and on payload while VALID, and latency bounds such as "READY within N cycles" or "every AR gets RLAST within M cycles". AXI sets no time limit; a master may even wait for BVALID before asserting BREADY (A3.3.1).

**Scoreboard:** data against a memory model with byte-lane and WSTRB handling (A3.4.3); per-ID ordering (same ID in order, any order across IDs, A5.3.1); expected responses (SLVERR or DECERR where the test provoked them, EXOKAY from an exclusive model). Error responses are legal, so the monitor reports them and the scoreboard judges them.

```systemverilog
// 4KB rule: INCR only, last byte measured from the aligned address (A3.4.1)
function automatic bit crosses_4kb(logic [63:0] a, logic [7:0] len, logic [2:0] size);
  logic [63:0] aligned = (a >> size) << size;
  logic [63:0] last    = aligned + ((64'(len) + 1) << size) - 1;
  return a[63:12] != last[63:12];
endfunction

a_ar_4kb: assert property (@(posedge aclk) disable iff (!aresetn)
  (arvalid && arburst == 2'b01) |-> !crosses_4kb(araddr, arlen, arsize));

covergroup cg_ar @(posedge aclk iff (arvalid && arready));
  cp_burst: coverpoint arburst { bins fixed = {0}; bins incr = {1}; bins wrap = {2}; }
  cp_len:   coverpoint arlen   { bins one = {0}; bins two = {1}; bins four = {3};
                                 bins eight = {7}; bins sixteen = {15};
                                 bins long_incr[4] = {[16:255]}; }
  cp_size:  coverpoint arsize  { bins s[] = {[0:3]}; }          // 64-bit data bus
  cp_align: coverpoint ((araddr & ((64'd1 << arsize) - 1)) != 0)
                               { bins aligned = {0}; bins unaligned = {1}; }
  x_burst_len: cross cp_burst, cp_len {
    ignore_bins wrap_illegal = binsof(cp_burst.wrap) && !binsof(cp_len) intersect {1, 3, 7, 15};
    ignore_bins fixed_long   = binsof(cp_burst.fixed) && binsof(cp_len.long_incr);
  }
endgroup
```

**Coverage to add beyond the AR group:** ID reuse and outstanding depth per ID; read-data interleaving across IDs; response type per channel; backpressure shapes (READY before VALID, VALID before READY, same cycle, long READY-low runs); W before AW, W with AW, W after AW; bursts that end exactly at a 4KB boundary; partial WSTRB patterns.

**Keeping it quiet on legal traffic:** run the checker against a known-good VIP-to-VIP environment with random backpressure first, and keep a list of legal-but-surprising cases as directed tests (READY withdrawn before VALID, W before AW, cross-ID reordering, error responses).

Read data with different ARIDs may return in any order and may interleave beat by beat, while same-ARID data must return in issue order (IHI0022E A5.3.1). A single FIFO of expected reads would flag every legal cross-ID reorder, so keep one queue per ID:

```systemverilog
// snippet: members of class axi_rd_scoreboard extends uvm_scoreboard
typedef struct { bit [63:0] addr; bit [2:0] size; bit [7:0] len; bit [1:0] burst; } rd_req_t;
rd_req_t     exp_q [bit [7:0]][$];   // per-ARID queue of outstanding reads
int unsigned beat_n[bit [7:0]];      // beats already received for the head of each queue

function void on_ar(bit [7:0] id, rd_req_t req);
  if (!exp_q.exists(id)) begin exp_q[id] = {}; beat_n[id] = 0; end
  exp_q[id].push_back(req);
endfunction

function void on_r(bit [7:0] id, bit [63:0] data, bit [1:0] resp, bit last);
  rd_req_t head;
  bit [63:0] addr;
  if (!exp_q.exists(id) || exp_q[id].size() == 0) begin
    `uvm_error("RID", $sformatf("R beat with RID %0h and no outstanding read", id))
    return;
  end
  head = exp_q[id][0];
  beat_n[id]++;
  addr = axi_beat_addr(head.addr, head.size, head.len, axi_burst_e'(head.burst), beat_n[id]);
  if (resp == 2'b00 && !mem.check_beat(addr, head.size, data))   // OKAY: compare data
    `uvm_error("RDATA", $sformatf("RID %0h beat %0d addr %0h: data mismatch", id, beat_n[id], addr))
  if (beat_n[id] == head.len + 1) begin
    if (!last) `uvm_error("RLAST", $sformatf("RID %0h: RLAST missing on final beat", id))
    void'(exp_q[id].pop_front());
    beat_n[id] = 0;
  end else if (last) begin
    `uvm_error("RLAST", $sformatf("RID %0h: RLAST on beat %0d of %0d", id, beat_n[id], head.len + 1))
    void'(exp_q[id].pop_front());
    beat_n[id] = 0;
  end
endfunction

function void check_phase(uvm_phase phase);
  foreach (exp_q[id])
    if (exp_q[id].size() != 0)
      `uvm_error("RPEND", $sformatf("RID %0h: %0d reads never answered", id, exp_q[id].size()))
endfunction
```

Here `axi_beat_addr` implements the A3.4.1 burst equations and `mem.check_beat` compares the active byte lanes against the memory model.

How each failure is caught: an **unknown RID** has an empty queue; **extra beats** show up as an unknown RID once the queue is empty, or as an early RLAST; a **missing RLAST** is caught on the final beat; **same-ID reordering** cannot be seen from RID alone, because both reads carry the same RID, so it is caught by the data comparison against the head's beat address. Read data for addresses that a write may still be changing is "either old or new" (A5.3.4), so the memory model must allow both or the test must avoid the overlap.

Responses are per beat (A3.4.4): skip the data comparison on SLVERR or DECERR beats and check the response against what the test provoked. On the slave side of an interconnect the RID is wider (the interconnect appends master-port bits, A5.3.5), so key the queues by the full ID there.

**No 4KB split for legal input.** A master must not start an incrementing burst that crosses a 1KB boundary (IHI0033C §3.6), and every 4KB boundary is a 1KB boundary, so AXI bursts made from a legal AHB burst never cross 4KB (IHI0022E A3.4.1). An AHB burst that crosses 1KB is illegal stimulus: flag it with an AHB-side checker rather than splitting it. The 1KB split belongs to the other direction: an AXI-to-AHB bridge must split a legal AXI INCR at each 1KB boundary.

**Known versus unknown length.** SINGLE, INCR4/8/16 and WRAP4/8/16 announce their length in HBURST (Table 3-4), so the bridge can drive AWLEN or ARLEN at once. An undefined-length INCR does not: the master may insert BUSY and then end the burst with NONSEQ or IDLE after any beat (§3.6.1). AXI needs the length in AWLEN/ARLEN up front and does not allow early termination (A3.4.1), so the bridge cannot simply forward the burst.

Strategies for undefined INCR (implementation choices):

1. **Simplest:** one AXI transfer per AHB beat (AWLEN = 0). Always correct, slow.

2. **Writes:** buffer AHB beats (the bridge acts as a posted-write slave) and issue an AXI burst when the buffer reaches a chosen chunk, the burst ends, or the 256-beat AXI4 INCR limit is hit.

3. **Reads:** issue a speculative longer read only where prefetching is safe (Normal, Modifiable memory); never for read-sensitive peripherals. Discard unused beats.

```systemverilog
// AHB HBURST -> beats known up front, or 0 for undefined-length INCR (IHI0033C Table 3-4)
function automatic int unsigned ahb_known_beats(logic [2:0] hburst);
  case (hburst)
    3'b000:         return 1;    // SINGLE
    3'b010, 3'b011: return 4;    // WRAP4, INCR4
    3'b100, 3'b101: return 8;    // WRAP8, INCR8
    3'b110, 3'b111: return 16;   // WRAP16, INCR16
    default:        return 0;    // 3'b001 INCR: length not known
  endcase
endfunction

// snippet: methods of the bridge's write path for an undefined-length INCR.
// on_ahb_write_beat() runs for each completed AHB write beat; flush() also runs when
// the burst ends (the next NONSEQ or IDLE is accepted). lanes() returns the WSTRB for
// beat i from the A3.4.1 byte-lane equations; send_aw()/send_w() drive the AXI channels.
task on_ahb_write_beat(logic [31:0] haddr, logic [2:0] hsize, logic [31:0] hwdata);
  if (buf_n == 0) buf_addr = haddr;
  wbuf[buf_n] = hwdata;
  buf_n++;
  if (buf_n == CHUNK) flush(hsize);              // CHUNK <= 256 (AXI4 INCR limit)
endtask

task flush(logic [2:0] hsize);
  if (buf_n == 0) return;
  send_aw(.addr(buf_addr), .len(buf_n - 1), .size(hsize), .burst(2'b01));  // AWLEN = beats - 1
  for (int i = 0; i < buf_n; i++)
    send_w(.data(wbuf[i]), .strb(lanes(buf_addr, hsize, i)), .last(i == buf_n - 1));
  buf_n = 0;
endtask
```

**Early termination.** AHB bursts can stop early: after an ERROR the master may cancel the remaining beats, and a multi-layer interconnect may end a burst (§3.6.2). If the bridge has already issued an AXI burst of known length, it must still complete it: send the remaining W beats with every WSTRB bit low, and accept and discard the remaining R beats (A3.4.1). Write errors on a posted write cannot be returned on the already-completed AHB transfer; log them or raise an interrupt. A non-posted bridge holds the last AHB beat (HREADYOUT low) until every split's BRESP is back, then answers with OKAY or the two-cycle ERROR.

**Width conversion** is the other reason to reshape: a 64-bit AHB transfer on a 32-bit AXI bus becomes two AXI transfers. A WRAP16 of 64-bit beats on a 32-bit AXI bus becomes 32 beats, more than an AXI WRAP allows (16), so the bridge issues one INCR burst if the start is at the wrap boundary, or two INCR bursts (start to top of block, then block base to start) otherwise.

Track, for every line the test touches, the state the model believes each cache holds and the latest value of the line. Use the ACE state names (UC, UD, SC, SD, I; they map one-to-one onto MOESI, but checking against the specification is easier with its own names).

```systemverilog
// snippet: members of class ace_coh_scoreboard extends uvm_scoreboard
typedef enum {I, UC, UD, SC, SD} ace_state_e;      // IHI0022E C1.2.3
localparam int NUM_CACHES = 4;
typedef bit [39:0] line_addr_t;                     // address without the line offset

typedef struct {
  ace_state_e st[NUM_CACHES];   // state the model believes each cache may hold
  bit [511:0] golden;           // latest value of the 64-byte line
} line_t;

line_t lines[line_addr_t];      // associative array: only lines the test touches

function void check_line(line_addr_t a);
  int n_valid = 0, n_unique = 0, n_dirty = 0;
  for (int c = 0; c < NUM_CACHES; c++) begin
    ace_state_e s = lines[a].st[c];
    if (s != I)            n_valid++;
    if (s inside {UC, UD}) n_unique++;
    if (s inside {UD, SD}) n_dirty++;
  end
  // A Unique line is in one cache only; a line in several caches is Shared in all.
  if (n_unique > 0 && n_valid > 1)
    `uvm_error("COH_UNIQUE", $sformatf("line %0h: Unique in one cache, valid in %0d", a, n_valid))
  // An updated line is Dirty in at most one cache.
  if (n_dirty > 1)
    `uvm_error("COH_DIRTY", $sformatf("line %0h: Dirty in %0d caches", a, n_dirty))
endfunction
```

**Updates from the bus.** On each completed transaction, set the initiator's state from the transaction type and RRESP[3:2] (IsShared, PassDirty; C3.2.1, e.g. Table C4-13 for CleanUnique), and each snooped cache's state from its snoop type and CRRESP (IsShared = 0 means it kept no copy; Table C5-8 for the read snoops). Call `check_line` after every update. Check R and CD data against `golden` (or memory when no cache is dirty), update `golden` on observed writes, and check write-back data on WriteBack/WriteClean.

**What the bus cannot show.** A cache may move UC to UD on a store and may drop a line without telling anyone (C1.2.3). So the model's state is an upper bound of what a cache **may** hold: a silent eviction makes "SC" possibly "I", which never breaks an invariant. If the design allows it, add a white-box probe of the cache tags to tighten the bound.

**Why the invariant matters.** A stale hit (UD in Core 0, SC in Core 1) produces no bus traffic at all, so a data check on the bus never fires. Only the state invariant catches it, at the moment the illegal combination appears.

**Races.** Two transactions to the same line are ordered by the interconnect, and the order is visible as response-before-snoop or snoop-before-response at each master (C6.2). Apply updates in that order.

**Coverage.** Cross transaction type x initiator start state x snooped start state x CRRESP (IsShared, PassDirty, DataTransfer), plus WriteBack-versus-snoop races (C5.2.3) and overlapping stores (C4.10).

**Two credit layers.** At the link layer a Transmitter needs an **L-Credit** for every flit; the Receiver grants credits with the channel's LCRDV signal, provides 1 to 15 of them, must be able to accept every flit it has credited, and must return credits without needing any action from the Transmitter (IHI0050E.b §14.2, §14.2.1). At the protocol layer, **P-Credits** let a Completer retry requests it cannot accept now (§2.11). A deadlock is a cycle of nodes each waiting for credits or buffer space that only the next node can free.

**How a loop forms.** Suppose an HN-F's snoop-response buffer is full and it stops returning RSP L-Credits until it can send more snoops, while an RN-F will not take a new snoop until it can send its snoop response. The cycle closes through the Home refusing the responses it is waiting for. The RN-F's wait is permitted: an RN may wait for progress on outbound RSP or DAT before progress on inbound SNP (§13.4.1). The Home's refusal is the design error: a Home must sink snoop responses. The specification lists channel rules only for Request and Subordinate Nodes, so treat this as a design requirement for the Home.

**The rules that break loops (§13.4.1, §4.11.1).** An RN must make progress on inbound RSP and DAT without needing progress on any other channel (so it accepts every Comp and CompData without sending CompAck first). It must make progress on inbound SNP without needing progress on outbound REQ. An RN-F answers snoops without any protocol-layer dependency on completion of its outstanding requests; the one exception is that it waits for all data packets of a pending request that has already received one. An SN may wait for outbound RSP before inbound REQ, but must progress on inbound REQ without needing outbound DAT, and on inbound DAT without needing any other channel.

**Assertions per link (simulation and formal):**

```systemverilog
// snippet: L-Credit bookkeeping at one channel's transmitter (IHI0050E.b §14.2.1)
// lcrdv: credit received this cycle; flitv: flit sent this cycle
int unsigned held;   // registered, so a credit is usable from the next cycle
always_ff @(posedge clk or negedge rst_n)
  if (!rst_n) held <= 0;
  else        held <= held + lcrdv - flitv;

a_flit_needs_credit: assert property (@(posedge clk) disable iff (!rst_n) flitv |-> held > 0);
a_at_most_15:        assert property (@(posedge clk) disable iff (!rst_n) held <= 15);

// Receiver side (design-specific): credits outstanding never exceed free buffer space
a_rx_room: assert property (@(posedge clk) disable iff (!rst_n)
  rx_credits_outstanding + rx_occupancy <= RX_DEPTH);
```

The model must also follow the link activation and deactivation handshake, which this sketch omits.

**Node-level dependency checks:** for an RN, an assertion or formal property that inbound RSP and DAT LCRDV keep flowing while outbound channels are stalled; for an RN-F, that a snoop is answered even when all its requests are outstanding.

**Liveness.** "For any reachable state there exists a sequence that returns a credit" is a branching-time property; SVA cannot express it. In formal, prove linear-time liveness under fairness assumptions on the environment:

```systemverilog
// The environment eventually returns credits on the Home's RSP channel
asm_rsp_fair: assume property (@(posedge clk) disable iff (!rst_n) s_eventually rsp_lcrdv);
// A tracked request (TxnID chosen symbolically by the tool) eventually completes
ast_done: assert property (@(posedge clk) disable iff (!rst_n)
  (req_flitv && req_txnid == SYM_TXNID) |-> s_eventually comp_for_sym_txnid);
```

Run it on a reduced configuration (two RN-Fs, one HN-F, one SN-F, shallow buffers). In simulation use bounded-response checks (`##[1:MAX]`) and label them integration policy, because CHI sets no time limits.
